WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:29.740
- Hi everyone, welcome to
the Homeroom livestream.

00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:31.150
Today, we're actually just going to have

00:00:31.150 --> 00:00:32.790
an "Ask Me Anything."

00:00:32.790 --> 00:00:36.020
So any questions you have
for me about anything,

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:38.760
I encourage you to put below,
whether you're watching this

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:42.150
on Facebook or YouTube, put
this on the message board below,

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:43.790
and we have team members
who will look at it

00:00:43.790 --> 00:00:45.830
and throw us those
questions about anything.

00:00:45.830 --> 00:00:47.250
So please start doing that,

00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:48.950
and now that I've given you a head start

00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:51.080
on asking questions, I will
give my standard announcements.

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:53.170
We are not-for-profit, the only reason why

00:00:53.170 --> 00:00:54.930
Khan Academy can even exist is because of

00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:57.070
philanthropic donations
from folks like yourself.

00:00:57.070 --> 00:00:58.710
So if you're in a position to do so,

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:00.700
please think about
donating to Khan Academy.

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:02.030
I do wanna give a special shout out

00:01:02.030 --> 00:01:04.000
to several corporations that stepped up,

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:05.710
especially as they saw
that we were already

00:01:05.710 --> 00:01:08.170
running at a deficit
pre-COVID, and then with COVID,

00:01:08.170 --> 00:01:09.810
our server cost went up.

00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:11.840
We are trying to accelerate
a whole series of programs,

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:14.300
so special thanks to Bank
of America, Google.org,

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:16.460
AT&amp;T, fastly and Novartis.

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:18.300
And that help has been a big deal,

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:20.110
but we are still running at a deficit.

00:01:20.110 --> 00:01:22.920
And so any support, whether
you're a corporation

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:25.810
or you're just an individual, any amount

00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:27.910
makes a huge difference
and allows us to reach

00:01:27.910 --> 00:01:29.900
a lot more folks.

00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:32.300
Another series of
announcements, I'll introduce

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:34.300
my colleague, Dan, that
some of you all have seen

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:35.250
before on the live stream.

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:37.680
Dan has some announcements as well.

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:39.240
- Hi everyone, thanks Sal.

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:40.410
So we have a couple of announcements

00:01:40.410 --> 00:01:42.560
and reminders for everyone for this week.

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:43.883
So today we just-

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:48.320
we recognize that summer's different

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:51.180
from the regular school year,
so we created a playlist

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:55.240
of recommended activities,
covering Math, Language,

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:56.920
Arts and Creativity.

00:01:56.920 --> 00:01:59.710
And in the Math section, I
just wanna call out two things.

00:01:59.710 --> 00:02:02.030
First Camp Khan as well as

00:02:02.030 --> 00:02:03.830
Get Ready for Grade Level Courses.

00:02:03.830 --> 00:02:05.430
So as a reminder, that we launched

00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:08.030
the Get Ready For Grade
Level Courses this week,

00:02:08.030 --> 00:02:10.460
and each of those Math
courses will contain

00:02:10.460 --> 00:02:12.450
the most important pre-requisite skills

00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:13.890
for that particular grade.

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:15.670
And we know you have a
limited amount of time

00:02:15.670 --> 00:02:17.570
so the intent is really
to provide learners

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:19.280
with the most efficient way to engage

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:21.510
with their upcoming grade level work

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:24.660
and wrapping the Get Ready
For Grade Level Courses

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:25.640
is Camp Khan.

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:28.090
So Camp Khan launched this Monday

00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:30.700
as a reminder it's our virtual
summer camp that kicked off

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:33.400
and it will run through the end of August.

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:35.760
Camp Khan helps students
prepare for Math in the fall

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:37.950
with those Get Ready
For Grade Level Courses

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:39.870
and Camp Khan was designed to build habits

00:02:39.870 --> 00:02:41.140
and mindsets for learning.

00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:42.360
And we were really intentional

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:44.220
about making it fun for the summer.

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:46.440
And so if you wanna learn
more, there's a parent webinar

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:50.980
all about Camp Khan tomorrow
at four o'clock to 4:30 PST,

00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:52.490
7 o'clock Eastern Time.

00:02:52.490 --> 00:02:55.130
So please join us if you wanna learn more.

00:02:55.130 --> 00:02:56.620
So those are the
announcements for this week.

00:02:56.620 --> 00:02:58.260
Thanks Sal.

00:02:58.260 --> 00:02:59.280
- Great, thanks Dan.

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:00.390
And I'll just doubly underline

00:03:00.390 --> 00:03:01.820
some of what Dan just mentioned.

00:03:01.820 --> 00:03:03.800
Obviously we had Lindsey on Monday

00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:05.560
talking about the Get Ready
For Grade Level Courses.

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:08.220
We're really excited about
this because in any summer,

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:10.260
a lot of students need,
you know, have gaps

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:11.310
that they need to fill in to be ready,

00:03:11.310 --> 00:03:14.870
and this is going to be a
summer, an extended summer

00:03:14.870 --> 00:03:17.670
where many, many more students
are going to need that help.

00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:20.330
And so whether you're a
student, a parent or a teacher,

00:03:20.330 --> 00:03:22.967
this is a great way for
students to get ready

00:03:22.967 --> 00:03:26.870
for grade level work and
make sure they have no

00:03:26.870 --> 00:03:28.210
Swiss cheese gaps going into it.

00:03:28.210 --> 00:03:31.200
And then Camp Khan, as Dan
just mentioned is a great way

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:33.910
of making it even more interactive and fun

00:03:33.910 --> 00:03:36.400
and having a whole layer
of programs and fun

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:38.530
on top of the Get Ready
For Grade Level Courses.

00:03:38.530 --> 00:03:42.490
So definitely check those things out.

00:03:42.490 --> 00:03:44.372
So with that, I'm happy
to take some questions,

00:03:44.372 --> 00:03:45.900
looks like the questions
have already come in.

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:47.787
Let's see from YouTube, Aya A says,

00:03:47.787 --> 00:03:50.620
"Hi Sal, do you know how
schools expect to open

00:03:50.620 --> 00:03:53.750
with COVID-19, if they do,
how will they do it safely,

00:03:53.750 --> 00:03:55.950
and if they don't, how can
they make sure we're learning?

00:03:55.950 --> 00:03:56.950
Thank you."

00:03:56.950 --> 00:04:00.310
Well, Aya A, I think what
you're referring to is...

00:04:01.270 --> 00:04:05.290
I think we talked to a lot
of district superintendents

00:04:05.290 --> 00:04:07.260
and principals and teachers,
and I think everyone

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:08.250
is really struggling with it

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:12.010
because I think even two
weeks ago before COVID-19

00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:13.780
started to pick again
and it does look like

00:04:13.780 --> 00:04:16.913
it's picking up again in
much of the US at least,

00:04:18.070 --> 00:04:19.293
people were thinking that,

00:04:19.293 --> 00:04:21.100
'All right, we're going
to have to do some type of

00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:24.890
socially distanced shift-based
model for back to school

00:04:24.890 --> 00:04:28.800
where half the kids come
on say Monday and Wednesday

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:30.870
and the other half come
on Tuesday and Thursday,

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:33.680
or maybe the high school
students don't come to school

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:35.510
and do everything virtually
and then they use that

00:04:35.510 --> 00:04:38.010
real estate for the middle
and elementary school

00:04:38.010 --> 00:04:40.380
so that people can become
more socially distant,

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:42.120
also do shift-based with the teacher."

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:43.900
So this is really complex thing

00:04:43.900 --> 00:04:44.787
that people were thinking about,

00:04:44.787 --> 00:04:47.160
and even before the pickup in COVID,

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:49.940
people were also expecting
a potential pickup in COVID.

00:04:49.940 --> 00:04:52.380
So you might have to
virtualize really fast

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:53.300
or go to Hybrid learning,

00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:54.730
or even if you are able to show up,

00:04:54.730 --> 00:04:56.430
there might be a subset of kids

00:04:56.430 --> 00:04:57.890
whose families don't feel comfortable

00:04:57.890 --> 00:04:58.723
sending them to school.

00:04:58.723 --> 00:05:00.923
So you're gonna have to
have some virtual model.

00:05:02.090 --> 00:05:04.150
What I'm hearing right
now, and this is, you know,

00:05:04.150 --> 00:05:06.060
a few days old, it might change

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:10.270
based on the epidemiological statistics

00:05:10.270 --> 00:05:12.900
of how far COVID's spread is.

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:14.900
People are definitely focused
on the younger students,

00:05:14.900 --> 00:05:16.010
'cause I think that's where, frankly,

00:05:16.010 --> 00:05:17.520
the child care issue is

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:19.610
super difficult for a lot of families.

00:05:19.610 --> 00:05:22.230
I think in areas where they can,

00:05:22.230 --> 00:05:24.820
people are thinking very
seriously about outdoors,

00:05:24.820 --> 00:05:26.380
you know, we had Rachel
Skipper on yesterday

00:05:26.380 --> 00:05:29.370
from Khan Lab School, and I
and her have been talking a lot

00:05:29.370 --> 00:05:31.560
about how can we leverage, cordon off

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:35.100
some of the parking lot and
essentially have some hot spots,

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:37.070
and leverage that for outdoor play,

00:05:37.070 --> 00:05:40.140
or even getting work done
for at least part of the day,

00:05:40.140 --> 00:05:41.500
to make it a little bit more safer

00:05:41.500 --> 00:05:43.490
and to get some social distancing.

00:05:43.490 --> 00:05:45.160
But the simple answer is, I think people

00:05:45.160 --> 00:05:47.080
are still struggling with can you do,

00:05:47.080 --> 00:05:51.960
even from a physical spacing
epidemiological point of view.

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:55.280
And then once you get that
right, given how uncertain it is

00:05:55.280 --> 00:05:58.580
the curricular point of
view is even more uncertain.

00:05:58.580 --> 00:06:01.000
This is why at Khan Academy
we are trying our best

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:05.530
to help support schools,
teachers, parents and students.

00:06:05.530 --> 00:06:06.580
You know, Dan's announcement about

00:06:06.580 --> 00:06:10.230
the Get Ready for Grade Level
course, that's part of it,

00:06:10.230 --> 00:06:12.160
to make sure that people are...

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:13.870
If they can use this over
the summer, that's great,

00:06:13.870 --> 00:06:15.610
they'll be more ready for back to school,

00:06:15.610 --> 00:06:17.520
but even if they don't
use it over the summer,

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:19.600
either the first couple
of weeks of back to school

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:21.970
could be focused on that
Get Ready for Grade Level,

00:06:21.970 --> 00:06:24.230
because a lot of kids have been disengaged

00:06:24.230 --> 00:06:25.470
for many months now.

00:06:25.470 --> 00:06:27.490
Or as you go through the grade level work,

00:06:27.490 --> 00:06:30.070
you can in parallel do the
Get Ready for Grade Level.

00:06:30.070 --> 00:06:32.700
We're also working on learning plans.

00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:34.010
Some of you all know several months ago

00:06:34.010 --> 00:06:36.610
we had learning plans for
learning, especially math,

00:06:36.610 --> 00:06:38.640
through the end of the
school year and the summer.

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:40.210
Now we wanna create learning plans

00:06:40.210 --> 00:06:43.100
for starting back to school
through the school year.

00:06:43.100 --> 00:06:46.320
So, expect to see those
in the next few weeks.

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:49.160
And we are actually working on a study

00:06:49.160 --> 00:06:51.760
of what were the best
practices of schools during,

00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:53.630
or districts during school closures,

00:06:53.630 --> 00:06:56.670
and given the best practices before,

00:06:56.670 --> 00:07:01.270
what are, kind of play books
for districts and schools

00:07:01.270 --> 00:07:03.810
to think about hybrid
learning, where you're doing

00:07:03.810 --> 00:07:05.770
some leveraging Khan Academy,

00:07:05.770 --> 00:07:07.700
where kids are learning at
their own time and pace,

00:07:07.700 --> 00:07:10.230
but you're also leveraging
synchronized sessions,

00:07:10.230 --> 00:07:12.600
whether it's socially distanced
inside of the classroom

00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:16.020
or synchronized sessions on
video conferencing of somehow,

00:07:16.020 --> 00:07:18.500
and how can you do that well,
what are the best practices.

00:07:18.500 --> 00:07:21.150
So, we're gonna try our
best to support teachers

00:07:21.150 --> 00:07:24.160
and districts in this
situation, but you know,

00:07:24.160 --> 00:07:25.010
the simple answer I have is

00:07:25.010 --> 00:07:27.154
I think very few people really know

00:07:27.154 --> 00:07:30.600
how this is going to evolve,
and it's a little scary

00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:31.920
'cause we're only about eight weeks away,

00:07:31.920 --> 00:07:33.870
and obviously if COVID keeps picking up,

00:07:33.870 --> 00:07:36.550
we might be back into a lockdown scenario

00:07:36.550 --> 00:07:37.383
in a couple of weeks.

00:07:37.383 --> 00:07:39.950
That isn't that different than what we saw

00:07:39.950 --> 00:07:41.650
in March and April and May.

00:07:41.650 --> 00:07:46.650
So, we're trying to do the
most on our side to help out,

00:07:46.980 --> 00:07:50.610
but it's gonna be tough and
uncertain for a lot of folks.

00:07:50.610 --> 00:07:55.157
So, let's see, from
YouTube, Osama Al-Sadi says,

00:07:55.157 --> 00:07:57.110
"Sal, do you have any plans
on improving your site

00:07:57.110 --> 00:07:59.080
and making new versions of
videos with better quality,

00:07:59.080 --> 00:08:00.910
and making new techniques for teaching,

00:08:00.910 --> 00:08:04.250
or making online paid courses
for example using Zoom?"

00:08:04.250 --> 00:08:05.720
Great question.

00:08:05.720 --> 00:08:07.700
So, the simple answers are yes!

00:08:07.700 --> 00:08:10.130
We're constantly thinking
about how to improve the site,

00:08:10.130 --> 00:08:12.440
and Khan Academy, you
know, we're over 200 folks.

00:08:12.440 --> 00:08:13.273
And I know some of you are gonna say,

00:08:13.273 --> 00:08:14.220
"200 folks!

00:08:14.220 --> 00:08:15.920
You know, that's a lot, we
can do a lot with that."

00:08:15.920 --> 00:08:19.210
But I remind folks, we're the
budget of a large high school

00:08:19.210 --> 00:08:22.310
and we're trying to, we have
107 million registered users,

00:08:22.310 --> 00:08:27.310
and we have 80, 90 million
minutes of learning per day

00:08:27.540 --> 00:08:29.090
during the school year, during week days.

00:08:29.090 --> 00:08:31.400
So, we're trying to do a lot, we cover...

00:08:32.290 --> 00:08:34.640
We have Khan Academy
Kids at the pre-K level,

00:08:34.640 --> 00:08:36.640
which covers math, reading, writing,

00:08:36.640 --> 00:08:37.600
social-emotional learning.

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:39.460
And then math, obviously,
we keep going all the way

00:08:39.460 --> 00:08:40.850
to early college.

00:08:40.850 --> 00:08:42.930
Then we have the sciences
at the high school

00:08:42.930 --> 00:08:45.460
and early college level,
we're adding English language,

00:08:45.460 --> 00:08:47.230
Arts, we have test prep, so
there's a lot of dimensions

00:08:47.230 --> 00:08:48.869
that we're working on.

00:08:48.869 --> 00:08:52.100
But we have a steady
pipeline of improvements.

00:08:52.100 --> 00:08:53.830
Some of them aren't as obvious,

00:08:53.830 --> 00:08:56.530
we're doing a major
infrastructure re-haul right now,

00:08:56.530 --> 00:08:59.300
which won't change your
user experience other than

00:08:59.300 --> 00:09:01.650
it will hopefully make
the site faster for you

00:09:01.650 --> 00:09:04.140
and more performant and
more reliable for you,

00:09:04.140 --> 00:09:06.040
and that also it'll make it easier for us

00:09:06.040 --> 00:09:07.380
to develop new features.

00:09:07.380 --> 00:09:09.840
Now, on the features side
we're constantly thinking about

00:09:09.840 --> 00:09:12.420
how do we change the user experience

00:09:12.420 --> 00:09:16.180
so that it is more engaging,
so that it's more effective.

00:09:16.180 --> 00:09:17.385
And so, you ask a lot about videos,

00:09:17.385 --> 00:09:20.830
so, I have a list of videos
that I'm either re-doing

00:09:20.830 --> 00:09:22.800
or new videos to cover certain topics

00:09:22.800 --> 00:09:24.300
where it seems like we still have gaps.

00:09:24.300 --> 00:09:27.000
We have a whole content team
that's constantly adding

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:31.480
and revising exercises, and
we have a large engineering

00:09:31.480 --> 00:09:33.770
and product team,
designers, product managers,

00:09:33.770 --> 00:09:36.670
to think about both the back-end,

00:09:36.670 --> 00:09:39.090
how do we make the
infrastructure more scalable

00:09:39.090 --> 00:09:40.840
as we're trying to serve so many people,

00:09:40.840 --> 00:09:44.580
and the front-end, which
in kind of tech-speak is

00:09:44.580 --> 00:09:46.130
the user experience side of things,

00:09:46.130 --> 00:09:47.920
how do we make it more engaging.

00:09:47.920 --> 00:09:50.130
And so, you see things like this

00:09:50.130 --> 00:09:51.670
Get Ready for Grade Level courses,

00:09:51.670 --> 00:09:53.470
that's more on the context side,

00:09:53.470 --> 00:09:55.030
we're doing these learning plans,

00:09:55.030 --> 00:09:57.750
that's kind of on the
content school-partnership

00:09:57.750 --> 00:09:59.100
side of things.

00:09:59.100 --> 00:10:02.084
And for this back to school
we're going to have things,

00:10:02.084 --> 00:10:03.710
you know we're gonna
bring back several reports

00:10:03.710 --> 00:10:06.920
that teachers have been asking
or improve those reports.

00:10:06.920 --> 00:10:09.340
And then I hope a few months
after that, you'll start seeing

00:10:09.340 --> 00:10:12.080
some navigational
improvements on the site.

00:10:12.080 --> 00:10:15.507
So there's a lot that we're
trying to juggle all right now.

00:10:15.507 --> 00:10:19.040
And I just kinda glazed
over the tip of the iceberg

00:10:19.040 --> 00:10:20.163
of what's going on.

00:10:24.010 --> 00:10:26.307
So Facebook, Carol Smith Capper says,

00:10:26.307 --> 00:10:29.170
"Sal do you have any
recommendation for older students

00:10:29.170 --> 00:10:30.530
who are supposed to be spending the summer

00:10:30.530 --> 00:10:31.690
doing internships?

00:10:31.690 --> 00:10:34.197
Since they have all been canceled."

00:10:35.380 --> 00:10:38.070
I do have advice and I
don't know how good it is,

00:10:38.070 --> 00:10:40.750
so take it or leave it, so to speak.

00:10:40.750 --> 00:10:42.610
But I've been telling
this to any young person

00:10:42.610 --> 00:10:45.040
who's in that situation.

00:10:45.040 --> 00:10:47.480
I think there's a ton
of opportunity right now

00:10:47.480 --> 00:10:49.930
to do really interesting things

00:10:49.930 --> 00:10:51.850
that can help a lot of folks.

00:10:51.850 --> 00:10:55.360
There could be simple
things of supporting people

00:10:55.360 --> 00:10:57.920
who live alone, especially elderly people,

00:10:57.920 --> 00:10:59.860
who might be feeling
very isolated right now

00:10:59.860 --> 00:11:04.040
in times of COVID and
helping them get tech access

00:11:04.040 --> 00:11:06.900
or through tech access, making
sure that they have someone

00:11:06.900 --> 00:11:08.950
to talk to, have someone to help them out

00:11:08.950 --> 00:11:11.560
with the groceries and it would be for you

00:11:11.560 --> 00:11:13.520
to figure out how to do that in kind of a

00:11:13.520 --> 00:11:16.140
safe socially distance way.

00:11:16.140 --> 00:11:20.770
I think there's a lot of
potentially the educators,

00:11:20.770 --> 00:11:23.730
teachers, who could help using support,

00:11:23.730 --> 00:11:25.920
especially as they are
trying to transition

00:11:25.920 --> 00:11:28.590
to this hybrid model
and teach kids over Zoom

00:11:28.590 --> 00:11:29.680
or whatever else.

00:11:29.680 --> 00:11:31.230
Maybe you could be a teaching assistant

00:11:31.230 --> 00:11:32.760
for them of some sort.

00:11:32.760 --> 00:11:34.873
We have this project schoolhouse.world,

00:11:36.080 --> 00:11:37.550
this is not a Khan Academy project,

00:11:37.550 --> 00:11:38.830
it's more of a Sal project.

00:11:38.830 --> 00:11:41.810
Maybe one day it will become
part of a Khan Academy project,

00:11:41.810 --> 00:11:44.550
but it is this notion that on Khan Academy

00:11:44.550 --> 00:11:46.820
you can learn at your own time,
pace, get practice feedback,

00:11:46.820 --> 00:11:49.200
gain mechanics, teachers can monitor it.

00:11:49.200 --> 00:11:51.800
There's videos and articles
to help unstick you

00:11:51.800 --> 00:11:53.763
and hints on each of the exercises.

00:11:54.880 --> 00:11:58.150
But a complete learning
experience would also involve

00:11:58.150 --> 00:12:02.720
some form of synchronous
live human interaction,

00:12:02.720 --> 00:12:04.220
where you can ask questions,

00:12:04.220 --> 00:12:05.150
where you can ask questions

00:12:05.150 --> 00:12:06.880
not only just from a teacher or a tutor,

00:12:06.880 --> 00:12:08.530
but maybe also from peers.

00:12:08.530 --> 00:12:10.610
And so this notion
about schoolhouse.world,

00:12:10.610 --> 00:12:13.490
which is Mariah Austin is a volunteer,

00:12:13.490 --> 00:12:16.130
who's been driving
this, and I've one of my

00:12:16.130 --> 00:12:18.300
buddies from college his
name's Shishir Mehrotra

00:12:18.300 --> 00:12:21.470
runs a company called Coda.io,

00:12:21.470 --> 00:12:23.010
they essentially volunteered their time

00:12:23.010 --> 00:12:25.480
to build the schoolhouse.world,

00:12:25.480 --> 00:12:29.260
so that it can match tutors
and students who need help,

00:12:29.260 --> 00:12:31.300
so that you can volunteer to be a tutor.

00:12:31.300 --> 00:12:33.669
But interestingly, right now

00:12:33.669 --> 00:12:35.330
it's a social engineering experiment

00:12:35.330 --> 00:12:36.163
that we're doing right now.

00:12:36.163 --> 00:12:38.040
We have more tutors and less demand.

00:12:38.040 --> 00:12:41.070
So if anyone has questions and
especially high school math,

00:12:41.070 --> 00:12:42.640
go to schoolhouse.world right now,

00:12:42.640 --> 00:12:43.920
there's a little registration form

00:12:43.920 --> 00:12:45.480
and then we let people in.

00:12:45.480 --> 00:12:47.920
So we're at this unusual
situation right now

00:12:47.920 --> 00:12:50.510
where there's some a lot of
amazing people who are hungry

00:12:50.510 --> 00:12:54.000
to tutor people for free as volunteers,

00:12:54.000 --> 00:12:56.880
and we're looking for more
students who need that tutoring.

00:12:56.880 --> 00:12:58.390
So definitely go to schoolhouse.world,

00:12:58.390 --> 00:12:59.670
whether you want to be a tutor

00:12:59.670 --> 00:13:01.470
or whether you want to learn something.

00:13:01.470 --> 00:13:04.670
I could imagine, there's a bunch of other,

00:13:04.670 --> 00:13:06.430
it might not even be
related to COVID right now,

00:13:06.430 --> 00:13:11.280
but this is a great time
on Zoom to start a podcast

00:13:11.280 --> 00:13:14.450
or an interview series on a
topic that you find interesting.

00:13:14.450 --> 00:13:17.720
And a lot of folks have more
time on their hands right now

00:13:17.720 --> 00:13:19.450
because they're at home
and they're not traveling

00:13:19.450 --> 00:13:20.283
and all of that.

00:13:20.283 --> 00:13:21.797
So you'd be surprised you
could reach out to folks

00:13:21.797 --> 00:13:24.270
and have interesting
conversations with them.

00:13:24.270 --> 00:13:27.300
I think it's interesting,
this is a family project.

00:13:27.300 --> 00:13:29.670
Get on Zoom with family
members all over the country

00:13:29.670 --> 00:13:33.430
or the world and record
your family history.

00:13:33.430 --> 00:13:34.890
That's not gonna be the type of internship

00:13:34.890 --> 00:13:36.830
that's gonna pay you, but I actually think

00:13:36.830 --> 00:13:39.490
it's pretty cool just to have...

00:13:39.490 --> 00:13:41.130
If you think about it, if
your house is burning down,

00:13:41.130 --> 00:13:44.780
God forbid, the thing that you're gonna

00:13:44.780 --> 00:13:47.820
try to leave the house
with are your pictures

00:13:47.820 --> 00:13:49.440
and all your family memories.

00:13:49.440 --> 00:13:50.850
And one thing that I've been trying to do

00:13:50.850 --> 00:13:53.910
is videotape family members,
especially older family members

00:13:53.910 --> 00:13:55.730
and ask them about their life history,

00:13:55.730 --> 00:13:57.060
ask them about their childhood,

00:13:57.060 --> 00:13:58.760
ask them about their journeys of life,

00:13:58.760 --> 00:14:02.350
the struggles, I get them on
video, you can record, Zoom,

00:14:02.350 --> 00:14:03.620
and Google Hangouts and all that.

00:14:03.620 --> 00:14:06.020
So I think that could also be
another interesting project.

00:14:06.020 --> 00:14:08.810
But there's tons of opportunity I think,

00:14:08.810 --> 00:14:11.873
to focus on whatever topic
is interesting to you.

00:14:13.960 --> 00:14:17.264
So let's see Adnan Kanchwala says,

00:14:17.264 --> 00:14:19.980
"Sir, you make all the
subjects very interesting."

00:14:19.980 --> 00:14:22.097
Thanks Adnan I appreciate that.

00:14:22.097 --> 00:14:25.340
"How did you find your love
for a particular subject?"

00:14:25.340 --> 00:14:26.741
Well, if your question is,

00:14:26.741 --> 00:14:28.950
how do I find the love for all subjects is

00:14:28.950 --> 00:14:31.910
because I do generally believe
all subjects are interesting.

00:14:31.910 --> 00:14:33.960
I've said this multiple
times on the live stream,

00:14:33.960 --> 00:14:36.467
that sometimes students feel like,

00:14:36.467 --> 00:14:40.003
"Oh, I gotta learn this
subject, I guess, I'll do it,

00:14:40.003 --> 00:14:41.380
what on the tests."

00:14:41.380 --> 00:14:44.140
We have to realize that
knowledge that you get to learn,

00:14:44.140 --> 00:14:45.430
Well, first of all, it's a luxury.

00:14:45.430 --> 00:14:48.460
If you think about like
human history, it's a luxury

00:14:48.460 --> 00:14:50.770
to be able to dedicate years of your life

00:14:50.770 --> 00:14:52.040
for the pursuit of learning.

00:14:52.040 --> 00:14:54.330
Most of human history, most human beings

00:14:54.330 --> 00:14:56.460
did not have that luxury.

00:14:56.460 --> 00:14:59.560
And that the learning that
we now have access to,

00:14:59.560 --> 00:15:02.180
we have access to the
total of human knowledge

00:15:02.180 --> 00:15:04.550
or most of it up until this point.

00:15:04.550 --> 00:15:09.450
And many very smart folks
would have done anything

00:15:09.450 --> 00:15:10.810
to have access to the knowledge

00:15:10.810 --> 00:15:12.440
that you now have access to.

00:15:12.440 --> 00:15:15.950
Now I do think sometimes
textbooks and some courses

00:15:15.950 --> 00:15:18.270
don't do the best job of reminding folks

00:15:18.270 --> 00:15:20.510
how exciting this
knowledge is and hopefully

00:15:20.510 --> 00:15:23.710
Khan Academy, at least, sometimes
if I'm making the video,

00:15:23.710 --> 00:15:25.063
you hear it even in my voice
that I'm excited about it,

00:15:25.063 --> 00:15:27.150
there's something interesting here.

00:15:27.150 --> 00:15:30.868
But I would remind yourself
about that in particular.

00:15:30.868 --> 00:15:35.868
If your question is, how
did I find the path in life?

00:15:35.920 --> 00:15:37.970
How did I decide what to focus on,

00:15:37.970 --> 00:15:42.090
given that I somehow found
every subject interesting?

00:15:42.090 --> 00:15:43.340
I mean, I used to say in college

00:15:43.340 --> 00:15:45.850
if there was something
called a PhD in everything,

00:15:45.850 --> 00:15:48.360
I would've tried to get that PhD,

00:15:48.360 --> 00:15:50.820
'cause I really enjoyed learning about

00:15:50.820 --> 00:15:53.560
a lot of varied things and
try to draw a connection

00:15:53.560 --> 00:15:54.670
between them.

00:15:54.670 --> 00:15:59.040
But for me, it was a combination
of I had a lot of debt,

00:15:59.040 --> 00:16:01.480
my family was not very wealthy.

00:16:01.480 --> 00:16:03.820
So when I was 21 years old,

00:16:03.820 --> 00:16:04.780
I was like, okay, let me do something

00:16:04.780 --> 00:16:06.390
that I find really interesting

00:16:06.390 --> 00:16:09.670
that I think I've developed
some really good skills at

00:16:09.670 --> 00:16:11.440
that can also help pay off debt,

00:16:11.440 --> 00:16:13.300
even help to support my family.

00:16:13.300 --> 00:16:17.070
And so this was in, I
graduated in 1998 from college

00:16:17.070 --> 00:16:20.080
and from a Master's program
in Computer Science.

00:16:20.080 --> 00:16:21.630
So it was a great time, and it frankly

00:16:21.630 --> 00:16:24.623
is even a better time now in that field.

00:16:25.460 --> 00:16:26.810
But as I went into that path,

00:16:26.810 --> 00:16:29.730
you know, I actually just gave
a few graduation addresses,

00:16:29.730 --> 00:16:32.070
and what I've told everyone
is when you're young,

00:16:32.070 --> 00:16:35.320
you often say, "Oh, do I pick
this major or that major?

00:16:35.320 --> 00:16:37.207
Do I pick that job or that job?"

00:16:38.243 --> 00:16:39.100
And ask a lot of people,

00:16:39.100 --> 00:16:41.260
make the best informed decision you can.

00:16:41.260 --> 00:16:43.300
But there's no such thing as
the perfect decision there,

00:16:43.300 --> 00:16:45.790
there's no such thing as the perfect path.

00:16:45.790 --> 00:16:49.190
What matters more is the
energy and the attitude

00:16:49.190 --> 00:16:50.950
that you bring to that path.

00:16:50.950 --> 00:16:52.170
So whatever it is.

00:16:52.170 --> 00:16:54.510
So I happened, my first job
while I was product manager

00:16:54.510 --> 00:16:56.110
at Oracle, which is a big

00:16:56.110 --> 00:16:58.650
kind of database enterprise
software company.

00:16:58.650 --> 00:17:02.710
And my boss told me,
"Look, Sal, it's easy to

00:17:02.710 --> 00:17:04.190
sometimes get cynical at a big company

00:17:04.190 --> 00:17:07.260
you feel you're kind of a
small cog in a big machine."

00:17:07.260 --> 00:17:09.440
But he's, like, "Just make
every day better than the last."

00:17:09.440 --> 00:17:11.350
How are you making yourself better?

00:17:11.350 --> 00:17:13.710
How are you making the
people around you better?

00:17:13.710 --> 00:17:15.130
Constantly be asking questions,

00:17:15.130 --> 00:17:16.810
constantly looking
around for opportunities

00:17:16.810 --> 00:17:17.780
for you to make a difference,

00:17:17.780 --> 00:17:19.600
whether it's at work or at home.

00:17:19.600 --> 00:17:21.460
And then my boss, after business school,

00:17:21.460 --> 00:17:22.820
I worked at a Hedge Fund.

00:17:22.820 --> 00:17:26.230
So as you can tell, I've done
my share of career changes,

00:17:26.230 --> 00:17:30.060
he reminded me that it's
super important as an investor

00:17:30.060 --> 00:17:33.840
to have time to pursue your
passions and develop interests,

00:17:33.840 --> 00:17:36.650
because it'll allow you to
see the world in new ways

00:17:36.650 --> 00:17:39.330
that other people participating
into the stock market

00:17:39.330 --> 00:17:41.230
do not, and so you might be able to see

00:17:41.230 --> 00:17:43.027
an interesting investment before others.

00:17:43.027 --> 00:17:45.080
And it also is just a way to recharge

00:17:45.080 --> 00:17:46.310
and make sure that you're not making

00:17:46.310 --> 00:17:47.760
a bunch of bad decisions,

00:17:47.760 --> 00:17:51.040
but more likely making
a few good decisions.

00:17:51.040 --> 00:17:52.150
So that's my best advice.

00:17:52.150 --> 00:17:54.270
Pick a path, ask a lot of questions,

00:17:54.270 --> 00:17:56.410
and, you know, you don't
have to be embarrassed

00:17:56.410 --> 00:17:58.050
about even asking about
very practical things,

00:17:58.050 --> 00:17:59.160
about how much money do you make,

00:17:59.160 --> 00:18:01.170
what are the work hours,
what are the benefits?

00:18:01.170 --> 00:18:02.960
But also this is going to be a job

00:18:02.960 --> 00:18:04.350
that I can enjoy doing,

00:18:04.350 --> 00:18:06.580
that I'm not going to be miserable doing.

00:18:06.580 --> 00:18:07.930
And then once you find that path,

00:18:07.930 --> 00:18:10.040
make sure you have space for your passion.

00:18:10.040 --> 00:18:12.440
And then you have this positive attitude

00:18:12.440 --> 00:18:15.480
towards both the job and
what you're able to do

00:18:15.480 --> 00:18:17.050
outside of your work.

00:18:17.050 --> 00:18:18.870
And the last thing I would
say, don't define yourself

00:18:18.870 --> 00:18:19.870
entirely by your work.

00:18:19.870 --> 00:18:22.900
I think in our society we kind of define

00:18:22.900 --> 00:18:24.760
who we are as people by our work,

00:18:24.760 --> 00:18:27.590
and then that creates
this kind of weird stress,

00:18:27.590 --> 00:18:30.610
and almost, it creates
unsatisfaction with work

00:18:30.610 --> 00:18:32.459
because you're looking for
almost too much meaning

00:18:32.459 --> 00:18:34.720
out of your work, where
you could a lot of meaning

00:18:34.720 --> 00:18:36.660
in other things in life.

00:18:36.660 --> 00:18:38.323
Let's see, good question, though.

00:18:40.460 --> 00:18:42.870
So Rylan Reid says, "I've used
Khan Academy for five years,

00:18:42.870 --> 00:18:44.140
and I have to say thank you-"

00:18:44.140 --> 00:18:46.477
Thank you Rylan for thanking us.

00:18:46.477 --> 00:18:48.480
"What is going to be your next update?

00:18:48.480 --> 00:18:50.370
What course or part of the
website are you most proud of?

00:18:50.370 --> 00:18:51.510
Thank you so much."

00:18:51.510 --> 00:18:53.740
Well, we've talked about
some of the updates,

00:18:53.740 --> 00:18:56.220
things like the Get Ready
for Grade Level courses.

00:18:56.220 --> 00:18:57.930
Expect to see things
like learning plans soon.

00:18:57.930 --> 00:19:01.690
We're constantly iterating and
tweaking all of the content

00:19:01.690 --> 00:19:02.763
across the site.

00:19:04.090 --> 00:19:05.330
There's gonna be some teacher tools

00:19:05.330 --> 00:19:07.800
that are gonna get
updated in the next few,

00:19:07.800 --> 00:19:10.210
actually, in time for back to school.

00:19:10.210 --> 00:19:12.920
We have some plans that are
probably about six months away,

00:19:12.920 --> 00:19:16.040
don't hold me to it, 'cause
there's still a lot going on,

00:19:16.040 --> 00:19:19.000
where we wanna improve the
navigation around course mastery

00:19:19.000 --> 00:19:20.293
and things like that.

00:19:22.068 --> 00:19:23.040
Those are the big ones.

00:19:23.040 --> 00:19:24.620
I mean, there's definitely
more subject areas

00:19:24.620 --> 00:19:27.070
that we wanna improve on,
so yeah, there's a lot.

00:19:28.200 --> 00:19:32.087
Let's see, from YouTube,
Sohana Anaya says,

00:19:32.087 --> 00:19:34.730
"I get forget math I've
practiced before very easily,

00:19:34.730 --> 00:19:36.740
and it's hard to remember
again in practice,

00:19:36.740 --> 00:19:38.060
how to remember it again in practice.

00:19:38.060 --> 00:19:40.550
So what should I do to never forget them?"

00:19:40.550 --> 00:19:42.520
Well, Sohana, my best advice,

00:19:42.520 --> 00:19:44.070
and this is actually
backed up by research,

00:19:44.070 --> 00:19:45.740
is this notion of spaced repetition,

00:19:45.740 --> 00:19:47.250
which Khan Academy tries to do,

00:19:47.250 --> 00:19:49.720
which is, when you learn something,

00:19:49.720 --> 00:19:50.960
there's kind of a forgetting curve,

00:19:50.960 --> 00:19:55.960
that over time, your likelihood
of being able to know it

00:19:56.600 --> 00:19:58.210
diminishes over time.

00:19:58.210 --> 00:20:01.350
But if after some degradation,

00:20:01.350 --> 00:20:02.790
you go and review that thing again,

00:20:02.790 --> 00:20:06.080
the probability is that it gets
back to where it was before,

00:20:06.080 --> 00:20:08.300
and now this time, because
of that space repetition,

00:20:08.300 --> 00:20:10.150
your degradation curve is slower.

00:20:10.150 --> 00:20:13.450
And so then, after say twice
as long, you review it again,

00:20:13.450 --> 00:20:16.230
then your degradation
curve will be even slower.

00:20:16.230 --> 00:20:18.730
So my best advice to you would be

00:20:18.730 --> 00:20:21.760
one, if you can create
a consistent practice,

00:20:21.760 --> 00:20:24.690
say even 15, 20 minutes a day,

00:20:24.690 --> 00:20:26.790
say three, four or five
days a week on Khan Academy,

00:20:26.790 --> 00:20:28.960
if you can make that a habit
just like brushing your teeth

00:20:28.960 --> 00:20:32.280
or making your bed, which
I also highly recommend,

00:20:32.280 --> 00:20:35.410
then I think it's much less likely

00:20:35.410 --> 00:20:37.350
that these skills will atrophy

00:20:37.350 --> 00:20:40.180
and that you will forget so to speak.

00:20:40.180 --> 00:20:42.100
And we have things like mastery challenges

00:20:42.100 --> 00:20:46.010
that make you review things,
do that space repetition.

00:20:46.010 --> 00:20:49.210
And we have things like course
challenges and unit tests.

00:20:49.210 --> 00:20:51.140
The other thing I would say
is when you're learning,

00:20:51.140 --> 00:20:54.920
especially math, sometimes
there's this tendency

00:20:54.920 --> 00:20:57.187
to memorize formulas and just say,

00:20:57.187 --> 00:21:00.720
"Okay, what steps do I need to
do to get the right answer?"

00:21:00.720 --> 00:21:03.090
That might work in the
short term sometimes,

00:21:03.090 --> 00:21:05.130
and that'll work less and less

00:21:05.130 --> 00:21:06.960
as you get into more
and more advanced math,

00:21:06.960 --> 00:21:08.727
what's far more helpful is to think about,

00:21:08.727 --> 00:21:10.210
"Why does it work?

00:21:10.210 --> 00:21:11.340
Why does it make sense?"

00:21:11.340 --> 00:21:14.610
That algebraic step it's
not some magic incantation.

00:21:14.610 --> 00:21:17.280
Why does it make sense that
you have to add the same thing

00:21:17.280 --> 00:21:18.390
to both sides of an equation?

00:21:18.390 --> 00:21:20.220
While two things were equal before,

00:21:20.220 --> 00:21:22.460
if you do something to
only one side of it,

00:21:22.460 --> 00:21:23.800
if you change the value of only one side,

00:21:23.800 --> 00:21:24.880
they can't be equal anymore.

00:21:24.880 --> 00:21:26.510
So you have to change
the value in the same way

00:21:26.510 --> 00:21:29.090
to both sides, so always
connect these dots.

00:21:29.090 --> 00:21:31.850
But what you'll find is
all of math is connected.

00:21:31.850 --> 00:21:33.040
And if you do that,

00:21:33.040 --> 00:21:35.110
especially with more of
the foundational skills,

00:21:35.110 --> 00:21:36.640
you're gonna find that
the more advanced skills

00:21:36.640 --> 00:21:37.660
almost become intuitive.

00:21:37.660 --> 00:21:38.493
You're gonna be like,

00:21:38.493 --> 00:21:40.270
"Well, why does someone
even have to tell me this?

00:21:40.270 --> 00:21:41.390
This is obvious."

00:21:41.390 --> 00:21:43.650
So I highly recommend,
do the space repetition

00:21:43.650 --> 00:21:46.280
and really sit and think and ponder

00:21:46.280 --> 00:21:47.960
why something in math makes sense

00:21:47.960 --> 00:21:50.000
and how it connects to
other things you know.

00:21:50.000 --> 00:21:51.470
And what I just said,
actually, isn't just math,

00:21:51.470 --> 00:21:53.500
this applies frankly to almost any field

00:21:53.500 --> 00:21:54.650
you're trying to study.

00:21:56.040 --> 00:22:01.040
So let's see, Rude Rainelle
Mandel from Facebook says,

00:22:01.167 --> 00:22:04.087
"How do you deal with
social media negativity?"

00:22:05.040 --> 00:22:09.930
It's a good question and I've
said on this forum before

00:22:09.930 --> 00:22:12.080
that I do things like meditation

00:22:12.080 --> 00:22:14.670
and how it's kind of
like Rain-X for the mind,

00:22:14.670 --> 00:22:15.503
for your emotions.

00:22:15.503 --> 00:22:16.980
Rain X, if you don't know, it's this thing

00:22:16.980 --> 00:22:19.190
that you can wipe onto your windshield

00:22:19.190 --> 00:22:22.220
and that when it rains,
the water just beads up

00:22:22.220 --> 00:22:23.053
and washes off.

00:22:23.053 --> 00:22:24.600
You almost don't even
need a windshield wiper

00:22:24.600 --> 00:22:25.900
if you put that chemical.

00:22:25.900 --> 00:22:27.730
I don't know if the chemical
is good for you or not,

00:22:27.730 --> 00:22:30.230
but it does wonders for beading up water.

00:22:30.230 --> 00:22:32.020
But I've always said
things like meditation

00:22:32.020 --> 00:22:34.250
are kind of like Rain X for your mind,

00:22:34.250 --> 00:22:38.060
because obviously if someone
says something to you

00:22:38.060 --> 00:22:41.300
that you disagree with,
or says, you're like this,

00:22:41.300 --> 00:22:42.830
or is making a generalization about you

00:22:42.830 --> 00:22:44.580
or say something negative,

00:22:44.580 --> 00:22:47.110
we all have an ego of different sizes

00:22:47.110 --> 00:22:48.403
that immediately wants to react to that.

00:22:48.403 --> 00:22:50.007
That immediately says, "I
can't believe they said that."

00:22:50.007 --> 00:22:51.577
"I can't believe they
think that about me."

00:22:51.577 --> 00:22:53.260
"Don't they know that I'm this, this, that

00:22:53.260 --> 00:22:54.540
or I'm a good person."

00:22:54.540 --> 00:22:57.230
We all have these internal
voices, which is really our ego.

00:22:57.230 --> 00:23:00.410
And when you meditate, you
realize you are not your ego,

00:23:00.410 --> 00:23:02.370
you are not that little voice

00:23:02.370 --> 00:23:04.260
that's always trying to defend itself.

00:23:04.260 --> 00:23:07.400
And then you can let these
things go a little bit more.

00:23:07.400 --> 00:23:10.430
It identifies your awareness
less with your persona.

00:23:10.430 --> 00:23:11.430
You're like, "You know what?

00:23:11.430 --> 00:23:13.380
They're criticizing this thing

00:23:13.380 --> 00:23:16.310
that they perceive called Sal or whatever,

00:23:16.310 --> 00:23:17.720
but I am not that,

00:23:17.720 --> 00:23:20.830
and really, should I get
that worked up about it?

00:23:20.830 --> 00:23:23.780
One day the sun will absorb the earth

00:23:23.780 --> 00:23:27.170
and the universe will
experience kind of a heat death.

00:23:27.170 --> 00:23:29.070
Is this really that big of a deal?"

00:23:29.070 --> 00:23:31.750
With that said, it does
hit you every now and then,

00:23:31.750 --> 00:23:36.750
and we're lucky at Khan Academy
because we are, I believe,

00:23:37.200 --> 00:23:40.990
doing a positive social thing
that most of the feedback

00:23:40.990 --> 00:23:44.740
we get is positive, but we do
get critiques and criticisms

00:23:44.740 --> 00:23:46.532
and every now and then I get an email,

00:23:46.532 --> 00:23:49.400
at one of my email
addresses at Khan email,

00:23:49.400 --> 00:23:53.010
which I do look at, which
are kind of angry sometimes.

00:23:53.010 --> 00:23:55.440
I suspect maybe a student
feels a little frustrated

00:23:55.440 --> 00:23:57.107
with the mastery mechanics or something.

00:23:57.107 --> 00:23:59.050
And they'll sometimes even use expletives,

00:23:59.050 --> 00:24:00.933
which I read those things.

00:24:02.240 --> 00:24:05.120
And it doesn't feel great,
but I just remind myself

00:24:05.120 --> 00:24:10.050
that it's not really
going at you directly,

00:24:10.050 --> 00:24:12.700
it's going at someone's
conception of what they think

00:24:12.700 --> 00:24:14.420
is some type of an organization.

00:24:14.420 --> 00:24:16.070
And I think it's much easier for people

00:24:16.070 --> 00:24:17.880
to be a little bit mean to what they think

00:24:17.880 --> 00:24:21.730
is an abstract organization
than to another human being.

00:24:21.730 --> 00:24:24.570
But yeah, so bottom line, meditate,

00:24:24.570 --> 00:24:26.100
try not to take that seriously,

00:24:26.100 --> 00:24:29.150
recognize that what folks
say about you on social media

00:24:29.150 --> 00:24:32.933
or anywhere else really isn't
defining of who you are.

00:24:34.400 --> 00:24:39.400
Let's see, so Michele Marie
Moussaoui says from Facebook,

00:24:40.527 --> 00:24:44.160
"Any advice on how to help
kids with math anxiety?"

00:24:44.160 --> 00:24:47.700
So I'm not a psychologist,
so take what I'm about to do

00:24:47.700 --> 00:24:48.533
with a grain of salt.

00:24:48.533 --> 00:24:49.670
But I saw this even in the early days

00:24:49.670 --> 00:24:51.220
when I was tutoring my cousins.

00:24:52.940 --> 00:24:54.760
I think there's something very powerful

00:24:54.760 --> 00:24:57.420
about building a habit, and
then as you build a habit

00:24:57.420 --> 00:24:58.287
and say, work on Khan Academy,

00:24:58.287 --> 00:25:00.433
and you start building some confidence,

00:25:01.390 --> 00:25:03.010
especially over the summer
for students able to work

00:25:03.010 --> 00:25:04.920
on Khan Academy, even 20 minutes a day,

00:25:04.920 --> 00:25:06.327
there's not the stresses of,

00:25:06.327 --> 00:25:08.750
"Oh, I have to get the
homework done by tomorrow."

00:25:08.750 --> 00:25:10.820
Or "How am I going to
perform on the exam."

00:25:10.820 --> 00:25:13.530
Or I wanna make sure that I
get an A or B in the class."

00:25:13.530 --> 00:25:14.900
None of those anxieties are there.

00:25:14.900 --> 00:25:17.400
So it's really you're just
doing it the way that you might,

00:25:17.400 --> 00:25:20.500
you know, learn a musical
instrument or practice a sport.

00:25:20.500 --> 00:25:23.160
And so, that I think in of itself,

00:25:23.160 --> 00:25:25.600
starts to reduce anxieties
and as the students practice

00:25:25.600 --> 00:25:28.540
in a kind of a deliberate
habit forming way

00:25:28.540 --> 00:25:30.900
that they will gain more confidence.

00:25:30.900 --> 00:25:32.370
And I recommend using the

00:25:32.370 --> 00:25:34.290
Get ready for grade level courses,

00:25:34.290 --> 00:25:36.810
or even start at kids, even if they're,

00:25:36.810 --> 00:25:38.430
a later elementary school
or middle school student,

00:25:38.430 --> 00:25:40.290
start them at early learning or start them

00:25:40.290 --> 00:25:42.270
in their arithmetic score
and arithmetic course,

00:25:42.270 --> 00:25:44.820
so they can review some of those basics

00:25:44.820 --> 00:25:47.779
and build that confidence
and then they'll fill to it,

00:25:47.779 --> 00:25:49.480
they'll learn all their Swiss Cheese gaps,

00:25:49.480 --> 00:25:52.290
which is another source
of lack of confidence,

00:25:52.290 --> 00:25:54.140
so that when they get to
their grade level or beyond,

00:25:54.140 --> 00:25:55.827
they feel a lot more confident.

00:25:55.827 --> 00:25:59.023
The other thing is, I think
it's reminding student

00:25:59.023 --> 00:26:01.500
just a little bit of that
meditation conversation I did,

00:26:01.500 --> 00:26:03.280
they actually even a minute
or two of meditation,

00:26:03.280 --> 00:26:05.433
which is really just sitting silently,

00:26:05.433 --> 00:26:09.080
and letting your thoughts
get as still as possible,

00:26:09.080 --> 00:26:12.080
I think is really powerful
for any type of anxiety.

00:26:12.080 --> 00:26:14.000
I've said it on this forum before,

00:26:14.000 --> 00:26:15.580
I've never really enjoyed planes,

00:26:15.580 --> 00:26:17.650
I get kind of claustrophobic on planes.

00:26:17.650 --> 00:26:19.220
It's like a real thing for me.

00:26:19.220 --> 00:26:24.220
And I just remind myself,
"Look Sal, that anxiety circuit

00:26:24.300 --> 00:26:27.400
in your brain that's starting
to cycle that's not you."

00:26:27.400 --> 00:26:29.150
And once you can kind of just observe that

00:26:29.150 --> 00:26:31.177
it starts to slow down and you're like,

00:26:31.177 --> 00:26:33.850
"Hey, I'm not this thing,
that anxious about this plane,

00:26:33.850 --> 00:26:37.450
and it is what it is,
the dice has been cast."

00:26:37.450 --> 00:26:40.020
It serves no one for me
to be miserable about this

00:26:40.020 --> 00:26:42.920
and I think even with young
kids, when you talk about that.

00:26:42.920 --> 00:26:44.620
And we talk about things
like growth mindset,

00:26:44.620 --> 00:26:47.110
that look, it's not about
whether you get it right

00:26:47.110 --> 00:26:49.000
or get it wrong, it's all about the effort

00:26:49.000 --> 00:26:49.833
that you put into it.

00:26:49.833 --> 00:26:53.210
It's not about whether
you're, "smart" or "not smart"

00:26:53.210 --> 00:26:55.960
I'd argue that's not the thing.

00:26:55.960 --> 00:26:59.520
It's about how much are
you willing to persevere

00:26:59.520 --> 00:27:01.750
and have resiliency and when
you get something wrong,

00:27:01.750 --> 00:27:03.290
learn from those mistakes.

00:27:03.290 --> 00:27:05.840
And that's actually when
your brain grows the most.

00:27:06.890 --> 00:27:07.823
Let's see,

00:27:10.830 --> 00:27:13.340
Other, there's a lot
of good questions here.

00:27:13.340 --> 00:27:16.900
So Susanna Garcia Domingos,
one of our regulars

00:27:16.900 --> 00:27:18.440
on the live stream good
to see you Susanna,

00:27:18.440 --> 00:27:19.750
hopefully one day we'll meet in person,

00:27:19.750 --> 00:27:22.227
I feel like I know since you
ask so many good questions.

00:27:22.227 --> 00:27:24.297
"What are some of your favorite movies?"

00:27:24.297 --> 00:27:26.770
And I think I've answered
that in a previous

00:27:26.770 --> 00:27:28.160
Ask Me Anything.

00:27:28.160 --> 00:27:31.000
I will say, you know, we
have Francis Ford Coppola

00:27:31.000 --> 00:27:34.040
on a couple of about two weeks ago,

00:27:34.040 --> 00:27:36.940
and I'm not usually a fan of bloody movies

00:27:36.940 --> 00:27:39.550
and violent movies, I'm
actually, I'm pretty,

00:27:39.550 --> 00:27:41.128
I guess you could say soft that way.

00:27:41.128 --> 00:27:43.753
I'm usually a fan of like
Pixar and things like that.

00:27:43.753 --> 00:27:47.410
But "The Godfather" is one
of the top movies of all time

00:27:47.410 --> 00:27:50.210
and so that's why it was
pretty cool to have Francis on.

00:27:51.150 --> 00:27:54.480
I think "Gandhi" is
definitely one of my favorite,

00:27:54.480 --> 00:27:56.220
when I was in college I used to organize

00:27:56.220 --> 00:27:59.153
an annual viewing of "Gandhi"

00:28:00.314 --> 00:28:03.683
so that's also one of
my top favorite movies.

00:28:05.240 --> 00:28:07.660
I will say "Star Wars" you know,

00:28:07.660 --> 00:28:12.660
episode four the original
one and the newer "Star Wars"

00:28:12.780 --> 00:28:14.670
feels like it's a little
bit of a business now

00:28:14.670 --> 00:28:17.470
where they keep kind of going
through the same plot line.

00:28:17.470 --> 00:28:19.950
And so it's kind of, I
don't know, you know,

00:28:19.950 --> 00:28:22.050
the plot line seems to be over and over,

00:28:22.050 --> 00:28:24.670
someone who needs to
discover their potential

00:28:24.670 --> 00:28:26.080
and struggle with the dark forest

00:28:26.080 --> 00:28:27.840
while the Empire is building some device

00:28:27.840 --> 00:28:28.940
that can kill planets.

00:28:30.360 --> 00:28:33.160
I think they're reusing that
plot a little bit too much.

00:28:33.160 --> 00:28:36.630
So the new "Star Wars"
in some ways, kind of,

00:28:36.630 --> 00:28:39.790
making the original "Star
Wars" feel derivative,

00:28:39.790 --> 00:28:40.930
even though it's the other way around.

00:28:40.930 --> 00:28:42.950
But "Star Wars" definitely is one of my

00:28:42.950 --> 00:28:44.853
all time favorite movies.

00:28:46.420 --> 00:28:48.909
Let's see other favorite movies,

00:28:48.909 --> 00:28:51.850
I could probably think of
others, but those are the three

00:28:51.850 --> 00:28:53.540
that are coming...

00:28:53.540 --> 00:28:55.010
And "The Matrix", "The Matrix" is,

00:28:55.010 --> 00:28:57.050
I remember when "The Matrix" came out.

00:28:57.050 --> 00:28:59.750
I believe it was the summer of 1998

00:28:59.750 --> 00:29:01.550
I had just I graduated from college

00:29:01.550 --> 00:29:04.550
and everyone was excited about
the new "Star Wars" movies

00:29:04.550 --> 00:29:05.383
that were going to come out,

00:29:05.383 --> 00:29:06.216
the prequels that were gonna come out

00:29:06.216 --> 00:29:07.890
at the end of that summer.

00:29:07.890 --> 00:29:09.930
And myself and a couple of friends

00:29:09.930 --> 00:29:11.460
we were just bored one
Friday, and we're like,

00:29:11.460 --> 00:29:12.710
"Well, there's this
movie with "The Matrix",

00:29:12.710 --> 00:29:14.930
it's got Keanu Reeves, you
know, maybe it's like "Speed"

00:29:14.930 --> 00:29:16.960
or something like that, and we went

00:29:16.960 --> 00:29:19.600
with fairly low expectations,
but we were blown away.

00:29:19.600 --> 00:29:22.010
So I'd say "The Matrix"
is a pretty good movie.

00:29:22.010 --> 00:29:23.890
And actually, "The Matrix"
is in a strange way,

00:29:23.890 --> 00:29:26.220
it's really connected to
these notions of meditation

00:29:26.220 --> 00:29:30.230
and what is reality anyway, and
should we get too caught up,

00:29:30.230 --> 00:29:31.790
Or, you know, to some degree...

00:29:32.840 --> 00:29:37.840
By definition, your mind has
constructed this the reality.

00:29:37.920 --> 00:29:40.130
Now, we all believe that
there's a true reality

00:29:40.130 --> 00:29:42.530
outside of our mind
that's informing our mind

00:29:42.530 --> 00:29:44.760
as we construct that reality.

00:29:44.760 --> 00:29:47.210
But "The Matrix" definitely
plays around with those ideas,

00:29:47.210 --> 00:29:50.010
I think, in a pretty effective way.

00:29:50.010 --> 00:29:54.563
So let's see, other questions.

00:29:55.830 --> 00:29:58.277
So Rubik's Nerd from YouTube says,

00:29:58.277 --> 00:30:00.800
"Can you please explain
Advanced Quadratics."

00:30:00.800 --> 00:30:02.490
This is probably not the
right place to do it,

00:30:02.490 --> 00:30:04.720
but we do have a lot of content

00:30:04.720 --> 00:30:06.930
on advanced quadratics on Khan Academy.

00:30:06.930 --> 00:30:09.150
I would go to Algebra II, and look at

00:30:09.150 --> 00:30:12.240
the factoring quadratics
or solving quadratics,

00:30:12.240 --> 00:30:17.040
or solving quadratic equations
on our Algebra II content,

00:30:17.040 --> 00:30:19.660
and maybe some of our
pre-calculus content as well,

00:30:19.660 --> 00:30:21.350
if you're looking at advanced quadratics.

00:30:21.350 --> 00:30:24.620
So, that's the best, we
probably have 20 or 30 videos

00:30:24.620 --> 00:30:28.180
and exercises just on
the topic of quadratics.

00:30:28.180 --> 00:30:29.680
So, let's see we're almost out of time,

00:30:29.680 --> 00:30:31.263
but I really enjoy doing this.

00:30:32.750 --> 00:30:36.450
Let's see, other questions.

00:30:36.450 --> 00:30:38.290
So, actually this is an
interesting one from Facebook,

00:30:38.290 --> 00:30:39.987
Mehran Ararat says,

00:30:39.987 --> 00:30:41.830
"Hey Sal, what is your opinion about IQ,

00:30:41.830 --> 00:30:42.980
Intelligence Quotient?"

00:30:44.240 --> 00:30:47.530
I am skeptical of it.

00:30:47.530 --> 00:30:50.800
I think you can have a
test with logic puzzles,

00:30:50.800 --> 00:30:53.650
and it will measure
capability in that dimension.

00:30:53.650 --> 00:30:56.500
It will measure your
capability at that moment

00:30:56.500 --> 00:30:58.370
in doing logic puzzles.

00:30:58.370 --> 00:31:02.898
But, the problem I have
with IQ tests is that,

00:31:02.898 --> 00:31:06.410
they'll say they'll use that,
how you happened to perform

00:31:06.410 --> 00:31:09.430
on those logic puzzles or
whatever they're making you do.

00:31:09.430 --> 00:31:11.360
At that moment and they'll
make some generalizations

00:31:11.360 --> 00:31:14.070
on your innate ability or
how that might carry over

00:31:14.070 --> 00:31:15.150
to all walks of life.

00:31:15.150 --> 00:31:17.680
And I think that just,
you're overgeneralizing

00:31:17.680 --> 00:31:18.513
at that point.

00:31:18.513 --> 00:31:20.720
For example, I think you
could take almost anyone

00:31:20.720 --> 00:31:22.670
and if you give them practice solving

00:31:22.670 --> 00:31:25.720
the types of logic puzzles
that you see on IQ tests,

00:31:25.720 --> 00:31:29.630
their IQ will increase, which
tells you that the IQ is not,

00:31:29.630 --> 00:31:32.603
their measured IQ would increase
according to those tests.

00:31:33.580 --> 00:31:37.510
And I know plenty of people
who, in that dimension of IQ,

00:31:37.510 --> 00:31:39.870
could do very well or
might not do so well,

00:31:39.870 --> 00:31:41.900
but in other dimensions
that I think any of us

00:31:41.900 --> 00:31:45.250
would consider a sign of intelligence,

00:31:45.250 --> 00:31:48.143
or sign of critical thinking
capability are amazing.

00:31:49.560 --> 00:31:52.350
I know some people who are amazing,

00:31:52.350 --> 00:31:54.290
they're amazingly funny or observant,

00:31:54.290 --> 00:31:58.000
or amazing public
speakers or storytellers,

00:31:58.000 --> 00:32:01.430
which I would say for me
it's almost the purist,

00:32:01.430 --> 00:32:03.120
someone is good at those things,

00:32:03.120 --> 00:32:05.220
that's kind of a raw form of intelligence.

00:32:06.270 --> 00:32:08.970
But, because of background or exposure,

00:32:08.970 --> 00:32:10.760
or just interest has never kind of gotten

00:32:10.760 --> 00:32:11.870
to the whole logic puzzles,

00:32:11.870 --> 00:32:13.920
which tend to show up in IQ tests.

00:32:13.920 --> 00:32:15.780
So yeah, they're interesting.

00:32:15.780 --> 00:32:18.180
I've taken them every now
and then, they're fun.

00:32:19.560 --> 00:32:21.230
I've been one of those
people who've been lucky

00:32:21.230 --> 00:32:22.450
to be good at those things.

00:32:22.450 --> 00:32:23.403
So, when I was young, I remember,

00:32:23.403 --> 00:32:25.970
I think it was second grade, I remember,

00:32:25.970 --> 00:32:27.640
some kind of a psychiatrist
sitting with me

00:32:27.640 --> 00:32:29.850
for like three hours giving
me a bunch of IQ tests.

00:32:29.850 --> 00:32:32.820
And then that helped me kind of get into,

00:32:32.820 --> 00:32:35.210
the school gave me more
attention, which was amazing.

00:32:35.210 --> 00:32:37.680
I was lucky, but I'm
suspicious of that test,

00:32:37.680 --> 00:32:39.700
'cause there's other kids
that probably had at least

00:32:39.700 --> 00:32:41.980
as much potential that probably didn't,

00:32:41.980 --> 00:32:43.830
that wasn't their thing.

00:32:43.830 --> 00:32:45.870
So, let's see other,
maybe we're out of time,

00:32:45.870 --> 00:32:49.880
but this is maybe, are there
any other last questions

00:32:49.880 --> 00:32:52.383
that maybe to finish off in.

00:33:01.030 --> 00:33:03.210
Actually, this is an interesting
question from YouTube,

00:33:03.210 --> 00:33:07.727
Adeline (indistinct) says,

00:33:07.727 --> 00:33:10.040
"Hello, Sal, right now I'm learning that

00:33:10.040 --> 00:33:12.920
all the Ivy League schools
aren't requiring the SAT,

00:33:12.920 --> 00:33:15.220
what then can you enter with?"

00:33:15.220 --> 00:33:16.640
So Adelaide, good question.

00:33:16.640 --> 00:33:18.670
So first of all, if they're
making the SAT optional,

00:33:18.670 --> 00:33:20.320
or the SAT or the SAT and ACT optional,

00:33:20.320 --> 00:33:23.480
doesn't mean that they're not
going to consider the SAT.

00:33:23.480 --> 00:33:24.470
I think the reason why they're doing that,

00:33:24.470 --> 00:33:27.310
especially this year
is because with COVID,

00:33:27.310 --> 00:33:28.960
access to taking some tests

00:33:28.960 --> 00:33:30.510
might be a little bit more difficult,

00:33:30.510 --> 00:33:32.710
but University of Chicago
and we had Jim Nondorf

00:33:32.710 --> 00:33:35.950
on this Homeroom a couple of months ago.

00:33:35.950 --> 00:33:37.460
They famously went testing optional,

00:33:37.460 --> 00:33:39.920
but still 90% of the students applying

00:33:39.920 --> 00:33:42.070
submit some form of SAT or ACT scores.

00:33:42.070 --> 00:33:45.510
So, the university is for sure
going to still consider it.

00:33:45.510 --> 00:33:47.010
And they do view it as something,

00:33:47.010 --> 00:33:49.120
once again they don't use your SAT scores

00:33:49.120 --> 00:33:52.010
defining of who you
are, but they view that

00:33:52.010 --> 00:33:54.472
in conjunction with your
grades, your extracurriculars,

00:33:54.472 --> 00:33:58.490
your essays, your
recommendation as something

00:33:58.490 --> 00:34:01.840
that altogether can help
predict your college readiness

00:34:01.840 --> 00:34:04.914
and your longterm potential.

00:34:04.914 --> 00:34:07.620
So, if you're in a position to
take something like the SAT,

00:34:07.620 --> 00:34:09.220
highly recommend to do it.

00:34:09.220 --> 00:34:11.430
Now, another thing I was
talking to Jim Nondorf about,

00:34:11.430 --> 00:34:14.427
this was kind of off camera, is he said,

00:34:14.427 --> 00:34:17.250
"Look, I would be
interested if kids can show

00:34:17.250 --> 00:34:19.610
that they've mastered
courses on Khan Academy,

00:34:19.610 --> 00:34:20.610
because not only does that show

00:34:20.610 --> 00:34:21.900
that they've know the material,

00:34:21.900 --> 00:34:24.060
but it also shows that they had the agency

00:34:24.060 --> 00:34:28.870
and they had the drive on their
own to learn the material."

00:34:28.870 --> 00:34:30.840
And so, I highly recommend
if you are thinking

00:34:30.840 --> 00:34:33.560
about going to selective universities,

00:34:33.560 --> 00:34:35.270
get mastery at your own time, pace,

00:34:35.270 --> 00:34:36.510
summer is a great time to do it,

00:34:36.510 --> 00:34:38.870
especially for some of you
had your internships canceled

00:34:38.870 --> 00:34:41.800
and use that time to master as
many of the advanced courses

00:34:41.800 --> 00:34:42.960
as you can on Khan Academy.

00:34:42.960 --> 00:34:46.770
Calculus, Statistics,
Physics, Chemistry, Biology,

00:34:46.770 --> 00:34:48.470
Economics, American history, Civics.

00:34:48.470 --> 00:34:50.286
These are all things that we have

00:34:50.286 --> 00:34:54.120
at a senior or even early college level.

00:34:54.120 --> 00:34:56.200
And if you can get 90%
mastery on Khan Academy,

00:34:56.200 --> 00:34:59.010
or a 100% mastery, take a screenshot,

00:34:59.010 --> 00:35:00.890
and send it in with your
college applications.

00:35:00.890 --> 00:35:04.900
I think, the colleges
will take that seriously

00:35:04.900 --> 00:35:06.660
and also tell them why you did that.

00:35:06.660 --> 00:35:08.770
'cause you have drive,
you have initiative.

00:35:08.770 --> 00:35:10.450
That's all the colleges
are really looking for.

00:35:10.450 --> 00:35:14.270
They're looking for good
people who are interesting,

00:35:14.270 --> 00:35:17.240
who wanna help the
world, who have the drive

00:35:17.240 --> 00:35:20.510
and the intellectual
capability, and the discipline

00:35:20.510 --> 00:35:23.940
to fulfill those ambitions
at some point in their life

00:35:23.940 --> 00:35:27.970
and also contribute to a diverse
kind of engaging community

00:35:27.970 --> 00:35:28.840
on campus.

00:35:28.840 --> 00:35:31.830
So, that's my best advice there.

00:35:31.830 --> 00:35:32.663
So, we're all out of time.

00:35:32.663 --> 00:35:33.750
This is super fun.

00:35:33.750 --> 00:35:35.360
We'll we'll do this again.

00:35:35.360 --> 00:35:37.873
And thanks everyone for joining.

